#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""harbormaster — caller-owned port leases: Firebase emulator sandboxes, Metro
ports, private simulators.

A localhost daemon hands each CALLER its own emulator sandbox (a pool port block +
fresh data) and reclaims it when the caller's process exits. No registry, no shared
per-project instances — two callers that both want "an impulse emulator" get two
independent suites, so they never collide on ports or data.

  acquire [--cwd DIR] [--owner PID] [--only a,b] [--ttl N] [--metro] [--sim]
          [--fn-env KEY=VAL ...] [--json|--env]
        Boot a private sandbox for the project in DIR (default: nearest firebase.json
        above $PWD). Owned by --owner (default: the calling process's parent), reaped
        when that process exits. Prints the ports (use --env to eval into your shell).
        --metro also reserves a Metro/Expo port (METRO_PORT). --sim also creates+boots
        a private iOS simulator (DETOX_UDID/DETOX_SIM_NAME) and implies --metro; the
        sim is deleted when the lease is reaped. --fn-env KEY=VAL (repeatable)
        injects env into the spawned emulator process — the functions runtime
        inherits it (e.g. LLM_CACHE_DIR for the e2e LLM fixture cache).
        Tune with --sim-device / --sim-runtime
        (or FBPORTS_SIM_DEVICE / FBPORTS_SIM_RUNTIME). This is the e2e dev-env: one
        lease = emulators + metro port + simulator, all torn down when the owner exits.
  up ...                 Alias for acquire (interactive dev; owner = your shell).
  down [--cwd DIR]       Release this shell's sandbox for DIR (else it's reaped on exit).
  release <leaseId>      Release a specific lease.
  status                 Everything the daemon has out.
  allocate [--owner PID|auto] [--ttl N] [--metro] [--sim] [--spawn CMD] [--cwd DIR]
           [--detach] [--json|--env]
        Reserve a block WITHOUT booting — for a caller that boots its own suite (the
        agent-qa run driver). Also owner-reaped. --metro/--sim work as on acquire
        (metro port in the block; private simulator, deleted on reap) for e2e Metro
        sessions whose emulators are per-RUN acquires. NOTE: reserved leases also
        reap on TTL even with a live owner — pass a long --ttl for long-lived
        Metro sessions (owner death still reaps promptly).
        --spawn CMD makes the DAEMON run CMD (via sh -c, in --cwd if given) as the
        lease's process: daemon-owned, so it survives the calling shell/agent —
        liveness is the spawned pid (+ TTL backstop), teardown kills its process
        group, stdout goes to the lease log. CMD sees FBPORTS_BLOCK_BASE,
        FBPORTS_LEASE_ID, and METRO_PORT / DETOX_UDID / DETOX_SIM_NAME when leased.
        With --spawn (or --detach) the lease defaults to NO owner pid, since the
        calling shell exits immediately and would reap it. Pass --owner auto to
        record the nearest ancestor that DOES outlive the call (under an agent,
        the agent process) so the lease dies with that session; or --owner PID
        to name one yourself. Otherwise release it with
        `harbormaster release <leaseId>` or let the TTL reap it.
        Any lease is also reaped once its --cwd stops existing — a deleted
        worktree can never need its Metro again.
  exec [--cwd DIR] [--only a,b] '<script>'
        Run `firebase emulators:exec` on a daemon-allocated block: boots the suite on
        free ports, runs the script (which gets FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST etc.), tears
        down. Use this instead of a bare `firebase emulators:exec` so concurrent runs
        don't collide.
  verify <leaseId>       Exit 0 iff the lease is FULLY alive: known to the daemon,
        its process running, its simulator still present, and (for booted suites)
        its ports open. The reuse gate for long-lived sessions:
            harbormaster verify "$FBPORTS_LEASE_ID" || <re-lease>
  doctor [--fix]         Everything alive in the world that the ledger doesn't
        account for: pool-port squatters, orphaned firebase runtimes (detached
        JVMs / functions workers re-parented to pid 1), stray run-*/alloc-* sims.
        --fix evicts them; exits 1 when findings remain unfixed. The daemon runs
        the same sweep itself at startup and every FBPORTS_SWEEP_INTERVAL (300s).
  serve                  Run the daemon (auto-starts on first command).

NOTE: firebase.json ports are optional — the daemon injects (and overrides) a
per-lease pool block, so concurrent suites never collide. Ledger:
~/.local/state/firebase-ports/ledger.json. See DESIGN.md.
"""

